.TH PITCH 1 "2026-11-22" "pitch 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pitch \- run a command at a higher or lower CPU frequency (the inverse of nice)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pitch
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ]...
.br
.B pitch
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.B \-p
.IR PID ...
.br
.B pitch \-p
.IR PID ...
.br
.B pitch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nice
changes how much CPU time a process gets.
.B pitch
changes how fast the CPU runs while the process has it.
It sets the process's scheduler
.IR "utilization clamps" ,
which the
.B schedutil
cpufreq governor uses to choose a frequency for whichever CPU the task runs on.
The clamp follows the task from core to core, so only that task (and its children) is affected.
.PP
.I N
runs from \-20 to 20, with the sign the opposite way round from nice: higher is faster.
The range follows nice's, so the familiar numbers mean what they suggest: +20 is full speed, and \-19 is the usual
background setting. \-20 sits one step below it, a lowest tier left for jobs an administrator deliberately ranks
below everything else.
.TP
.B +1 .. +20
raise the clamp floor,
.BR uclamp.min " = " N /20 \(mu 1024.
The CPU clocks as if the task kept it at least that busy, so short-lived and serial work (configure scripts,
compiler invocations, package builds) runs at speed instead of starting at the lowest frequency each time.
+20 asks for the top frequency whenever the task runs; +10 for about 60 % of it with schedutil's 25 % headroom.
.TP
.B \-1 .. \-20
lower the clamp ceiling,
.BR uclamp.max " = (20 + " N ")/20 \(mu 1024."
The task never asks for more than that share of the top frequency: background work such as mirror syncs or
batch jobs runs slower and at better energy per operation. \-20 holds it at the lowest frequency.
.TP
.B 0
restore the default (the task follows its cgroup and the system defaults).
.PP
Without
.BR \-n ,
a command runs at +20.
The scale is linear in clamp units, but its effect is not: a floor only matters above the frequency schedutil would
pick anyway, and a ceiling only below it, so the ends of the range do most of the work (see
.BR NOTES ).
Without a command,
.B pitch
prints its own pitch (like
.B nice
prints its niceness), which is the pitch it inherited.
.PP
Children inherit the pitch across
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR execve (2).
Unlike nice, the value is absolute, not an adjustment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-pitch " " \fIN\fR
The pitch, \-20 to 20.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background
The background tier, in one flag: besides the pitch, switch the task to
.B SCHED_IDLE
(it gets CPU time only when nothing else wants it, as
.BR "chrt \-\-idle 0" )
and the idle I/O class (disk time only when nobody else wants it, as
.BR "ionice \-c3" ).
The pitch defaults to \-20, so the job also leaves the package power budget to the foreground;
.B \-b \-n 20
instead runs it on spare capacity at full speed.
Children inherit all three, and any user may apply them to their own processes.
cgroup limits (CPU weight, memory) stay with
.BR systemd\-run (1);
see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid
Operate on the listed process IDs: with
.B \-n
or
.BR \-b ,
set every thread of each process; without them, show each process's pitch, its requested and effective clamps,
scheduling policy and I/O class.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No warnings.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Version.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
A kernel with utilization clamping
.RB ( CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK ,
present in Ubuntu and most distribution kernels) and the
.B schedutil
governor.
Other governors ignore clamps;
.B pitch
then warns, names the fix for the cpufreq driver in use, and changes nothing that matters:
.TP
.B intel_pstate \fR(active, the default on Intel CPUs with HWP)
boot with
.B intel_pstate=passive cpufreq.default_governor=schedutil
(the active driver offers only powersave and performance).
.TP
.B amd\-pstate\-epp \fR(active, the default on recent AMD CPUs)
boot with
.B amd_pstate=passive
(or
.BR guided )
.BR cpufreq.default_governor=schedutil .
.TP
.B intel_cpufreq\fR, \fBamd\-pstate\fR, \fBacpi\-cpufreq\fR, \fBcppc_cpufreq\fR, ...
select schedutil:
.B cpufreq.default_governor=schedutil
at boot, or write it to
.IR /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy*/scaling_governor .
On ARM big.LITTLE systems the clamps also steer placement: boosted tasks go to big cores, capped ones to little cores.
.PP
Built and tested on x86_64; aarch64, riscv64 and loongarch64 have the system call numbers but are untested.
.PP
With schedutil on Linux, set
.B kernel.sched_util_clamp_min_rt_default=0
(sysctl). Otherwise every runnable realtime task requests the top frequency, and on server CPUs
whose uncore clock follows the highest request a single periodic realtime task can hold it at maximum:
measured at +72 to +76 W at idle on a 4-socket Xeon.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Any user may set the pitch of their own processes, in both directions; root may set any process's.
A cgroup's
.B cpu.uclamp.min
and
.B cpu.uclamp.max
bound what its tasks get, and
.B kernel.sched_util_clamp_min
and
.B kernel.sched_util_clamp_max
bound every task on the system.
.B pitch
warns when the system limit is below a requested boost.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.I COMMAND
when it runs.
125 when
.B pitch
itself fails (usage),
126 when
.I COMMAND
is found but cannot be run,
127 when it is not found.
If the pitch cannot be set,
.B pitch
reports it and runs
.I COMMAND
anyway, as nice does.
With
.BR \-p ,
1 when any process could not be read or set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The quickest way to get a feel for it: one long CPU-bound job (it prints a short message) under different ceilings.
Measured on a Xeon Platinum 8280L, 3.9 GHz single-core turbo, 1.0 GHz minimum:
.PP
.nf
.RS
time pitch \-n 0   \-\- perl \-le'print Zsb1e6pzo^chr((grep{(1x$_)!~/^(11+)\e1+$/}2..2e4)%32)x9'    # 17.1 s
time pitch \-n \-10 \-\- perl \-le'print Zsb1e6pzo^chr((grep{(1x$_)!~/^(11+)\e1+$/}2..2e4)%32)x9'    # 32.4 s
time pitch \-n \-20 \-\- perl \-le'print Zsb1e6pzo^chr((grep{(1x$_)!~/^(11+)\e1+$/}2..2e4)%32)x9'    # 65.8 s
.RE
.fi
.PP
\-20 takes 3.8 times as long: turbo over minimum frequency.
.B pitch \-n 20
gives 17.0 s, since a long-running job already gets full speed; a boost shows on short, bursty work.
.TP
.B pitch \-n 20 \-\- dpkg-buildpackage \-b
A package build at full speed. Measured on a PowerEdge R940xa (schedutil): 18.0 s instead of 21.3 to 22.4 s, with
12 % less package energy.
.TP
.B pitch \-n \-15 \-\- rsync \-a mirror/ /srv/mirror/
A background sync that stays near the lowest frequency.
.TP
.B pitch \-b \-\- job
The background tier: idle CPU scheduling, idle I/O and the lowest frequency, in one flag.
.PP
A full background tier adds the cgroup limits, which belong to systemd:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo systemd\-run \-\-scope \-p CPUWeight=1 \-p MemoryHigh=2G pitch \-b \-\- job
.RE
.fi
.IP
.B CPUWeight=1
and
.B SCHED_IDLE
mean the job only gets cores nothing else wants;
.B MemoryHigh
bounds its memory; the idle I/O class gives it disk time last; \-20 keeps its cores near the lowest frequency,
leaving the package power budget to the foreground tasks' turbo (with
.B \-b \-n 20
instead, it finishes sooner on spare capacity at the cost of that power).
The same without
.BR \-b :
.BR "chrt \-\-idle 0 ionice \-c3 pitch \-n \-20 \-\- job" .
Without
.BR sudo ,
a
.B \-\-user
scope's weight only competes with the user's own processes. Add
.B IOWeight=1
only where the disk uses the BFQ scheduler or io.cost (it does nothing under mq\-deadline or none).
.TP
.B pitch \-n 20 \-p $(pgrep qemu\-system)
Boost running virtual machines (every vCPU thread).
.TP
.B pitch \-p $$
Show the shell's pitch.
.SH NOTES
Measured on a PowerEdge R940xa (4\(mu Xeon Platinum 8280L, schedutil, Hyper-Threading on):
.IP \(bu 2
Serial package build: +5 and +10 changed little (0 to \-2 %), +15 \-7 %, +18 and +20 \-15 % time and \-11 % package
energy. Parallel builds that already load every core: no change.
.IP \(bu 2
Background work at a fixed rate (25 % of the machine): \-20 drew 67 W less at the wall for the same work.
.IP \(bu 2
A pitch is not a share of CPU time. A capped job keeps its core busy longer, so where cores are scarce it holds them
longer; use
.B \-b
(or
.BR "chrt \-\-idle" )
to yield CPU time as well.
.IP \(bu 2
Both hardware threads of a core run at one frequency, the higher of their requests: a capped task on the sibling of a
boosted one runs fast anyway.
.IP \(bu 2
Do not give long-running, mostly idle services a high pitch: every wake-up requests full speed, and on server CPUs
whose uncore clock follows the highest request that costs power at idle (+11 to +16 W for one 100 ms periodic task at
+20 on the R940xa). Use it for jobs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nice (1),
.BR renice (1),
.BR uclampset (1),
.BR chrt (1),
.BR ionice (1),
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR sched_setattr (2),
.BR sched (7)
